## Choose the colour mapping

| Relationship | Meaning of colour | Required context |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Categorical | Identifies a different source or device | Stable identifier, readable name and another cue such as a line pattern or marker |
| Sequential | Moves in one ordered direction as a value increases | Measure, unit, minimum, maximum, direction and explicit handling outside the scale |
| Divergent | Shows direction and magnitude away from a meaningful centre | A justified centre, two declared endpoints, signed values and a definition of each direction |
| Missing | There is no usable numeric observation | A label such as “Not supplied”, a non-numeric mark and an explanation where needed |

A categorical colour is not a rank or amount. Do not use generated device colours as a temperature progression. A divergent centre must represent something meaningful, such as no difference from a stated reference; it is not simply the midpoint that makes a palette look balanced. An actual zero belongs on the numeric scale. Unknown and unavailable observations do not.

The [GOV.UK chart colour guidance](https://brand.design-system.service.gov.uk/colour/charts/) recommends a maximum of **two colour scales in one chart** when using sequential or divergent scales. This is not a limit of two categorical series. Split the graphic or use a table when additional measures make the colour meaning difficult to follow.

## Define and test a quantitative scale

Declare the quantity, units, period, endpoints and any centre before selecting colours. Give an out-of-range value an explicit explanation; silently assigning it the endpoint colour hides information. If the range changes, update the legend and explain whether charts across different periods remain comparable.

The example uses simple, rounded interpolation of encoded sRGB channels. Its sequential endpoints are `#D2E2F1` and `#103E65`. Its divergent branches run from `#1D70B8` through the neutral `#F3F3F3` to `#8A6100`. These are fixed, opaque **reference colours**, not another categorical palette, production tokens or a perceptually uniform scale. Intermediate hex values are calculated from the documented inputs. A real heatmap or spatial layer needs its own reviewed range, context and contrast evidence.

Keep numeric and categorical labels outside fills unless the actual text/fill combination has been tested. Pale scale steps may not contrast with a white canvas. Use visible boundaries and direct labels or provide a simpler view; never claim that distinct hex values prove distinguishable adjacent areas. The worked examples use outlines, written values and matching tables. The [GOV.UK map colour guidance](https://brand.design-system.service.gov.uk/colour/maps/) also supports shapes, labels and alternative representations where colour contrast is insufficient.

Missing values use a crossed empty swatch and the words “Not supplied”. Its absence of a numeric colour is deliberate. At forced colours or with fills removed, the labels and data tables still convey every value and its missing state.


## Generate from identity

Pass a stable native source or statistic ID to `chartColour(key, {tone, mode})`. The generator calculates an opaque sRGB colour through OKLCH, a colour space that separates lightness, colour strength and hue. It reduces colour strength when necessary to fit the screen gamut and adjusts lightness to meet the chart-surface contrast target. Muted means lower colour strength, **not transparency**.

The result is deterministic. The same identity and display settings give the same colour across reloads, chart ordering and legend changes. It does not require a saved per-device colour map or a fixed list of 20 colours. Keep the identifier stable when a friendly name changes. Previous-period comparisons and the positive/negative halves of a native power source share their base identity.

Semantic energy roles reserve recognisable families: grid import, grid export, solar, gas, water, battery charging, battery discharge and non-fossil energy. Total consumption has a neutral role and a distinct dashed line. Multiple meters use namespaced keys such as `gas:<statistic ID>` to retain a family resemblance while adding stable variation. Unrelated devices use the wider generated hue range.

The theme also supplies **24 fallback colour variables**, `civica-chart-1` to `civica-chart-24`, for consumers that only accept numbered variables. These are compatibility slots, not a maximum series count. Identity-aware charts use the generator directly. The first eight slots retain their existing energy-role mapping.

## Vivid, Muted, Light and Dark

| Setting | What changes | What stays stable |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Brand palette | Branding and interface accents | Chart identity and energy family |
| Vivid / Muted | Chart colour strength and tested lightness | Identity, family and patterns |
| Light / Dark | Contrast against the actual chart surface | Identity, family and patterns |
| Hide, reorder or add a series | Native visibility or ordering | Colours of existing identities |

All chart fills and strokes use opaque colours. Do not append alpha bytes, use a gradient, or reduce a series' opacity to create a second palette. Hover must not thicken a line or fade every other series. Normal, hover and legend lines use the shared **1.5px** adapter constant across tones and modes; this is the [documented adaptation](../components/charts.html#visual-rules) of the official ONS 3px reference. A previous-period column uses the same identity colour with a hatch pattern, with its original date available in the data table.

## Support colour with another cue

Use `chartSeriesStyle` for the same colour plus deterministic line dashes, marker identity and fill-pattern choices. The adapter applies matching keys to native legend items while retaining their visibility controls and accessible labels. Use readable source names, a distinct total line, explicit comparison labels and the **View data** table.

A range of 20 or more generated colours is useful for large device inventories. It is **not a claim that 20 simultaneous colours are easy to distinguish**, including for people with colour-vision differences. Patterns also have finite perceptual capacity. Prefer fewer visible series, native legend isolation and device detail where available. Never silently delete observations or merge devices to meet a suggested limit. A 3:1 contrast check against the canvas does not establish separation between every pair of colours.

The 24-device example makes this limitation visible. Check labels and patterns as well as swatches; do not approve a palette merely because its hex strings are unique. At crowded sizes, retain all values in the table and allow native controls to focus the chart.

## Maintenance and evidence

The algorithm version, semantic families, contrast surfaces, pattern choices and example identities are recorded in the JSON contract. A change to the algorithm may change existing identity colours and must be versioned and explained. Run palette, native-shaped renderer and live-route checks before release.

For the documentation references, `docs/data/data-colour-examples.yml` records the fixed IDs, original values, generated context mappings, generator hash and quantitative formulas under `colour_reference`. Check those records against the visible swatches and tables. Regenerate the categorical references after an algorithm change; do not hand-edit individual hex values. These static examples contain no theme or device controls and do not require JavaScript.

The GOV.UK [data](https://brand.design-system.service.gov.uk/data/), [chart colour](https://brand.design-system.service.gov.uk/colour/charts/) and [map colour](https://brand.design-system.service.gov.uk/colour/maps/) pages were rechecked on **12 November 2026**. Their chart palette is source guidance, not a replacement for the existing civica.uk identity generator. The operational **1.5px**, unfilled-line and stable-colour contracts above are unchanged.

This is a civica.uk extension to ONS guidance, not an official ONS palette. ONS recommends limiting categories and using colour consistently: see [using colours in charts](https://service-manual.ons.gov.uk/data-visualisation/colours/using-colours-in-charts). Our generated range supports Home Assistant's larger inventories while retaining that guidance about readability.
